      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "When performing intravascular ultrasound studies, the backward echo image can show marked attenuation, although there are no calcified deposits and it may be impossible to detect the intraplaque architecture. The pathology underlying this phenomenon was investigated in autopsy specimens. We hypothesize that the mechanism responsible for the attenuation involves micro-calcification and lipid in unstable plaques causing ultrasonic wave reflection and dispersion."},
